{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Status: census complete, this ticket is now remediation\n\nThe investigation this ticket originally planned has been performed and is recorded in full in the comments. The census is established fact; what remains is the fix.\n\n## Established findings\n\n`make test` under the agent sandbox on unmodified `origin/main` fails **51** tests, reproducibly, with no nondeterminism across repeated runs. Of those, **4** are the POSIX named-semaphore denials owned by `0eba-cfd8-34b0-4cb7` and out of scope here. The **47** remaining decompose into **5 distinct causes**, every one probe-confirmed:\n\n| Cause | Count | Mechanism | Sandbox-specific? |\n|---|---:|---|---|\n| Git identity | 20 | The harness injects `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=8` with command-line `user.name`/`user.email` that outrank repo-local config, alongside a fake `HOME` | Yes — harness artifact |\n| pandoc | 17 | The bundled `pypandoc` binary is Mach-O **x86_64 on an arm64 host** (`Errno 86, Bad CPU type`), and no system pandoc is present | **No** — breaks for any Apple Silicon developer |\n| semgrep | 8 | Writing the real-home `~/.semgrep/settings.yml` is denied | Yes |\n| `ps` | 1 | Process enumeration denied | Yes — the only genuine sandbox denial |\n| Close gate | 1 | Ordinary baseline defect, unrelated to environment | No — split to `9753-647b-2f85-40ef` |\n\n**`make verify` can be made green under the sandbox.** There is no irreducible residue: every one of the 47 has a remedy that does not degrade the gate. That verdict is what makes this ticket worth doing rather than documenting as a limitation.\n\nTwo findings reframe the problem materially:\n\n- **The pandoc failures are not a sandbox problem.** `shutil.which` reports a binary that cannot execute on the host architecture. This breaks `make verify` for every Apple Silicon developer, sandboxed or not. It is a genuine repository defect that the sandbox investigation happened to surface.\n- **Worker count is not a remedy for any part of this.** The runner-level (pytest-xdist) failure population measured exactly **0**; xdist brings its pool up successfully and reaches 99% of the suite.\n\n## Approach\n\nRemediation is centralized in `tests/conftest.py` so the treatment of every cause is uniform and visible in one place.\n\n1. **Git identity (20).** Scrub the injected `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT` / `GIT_CONFIG_KEY_n` / `GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_n` triple from the environment for tests that construct their own repositories, so repo-local config governs as the tests intend. Measured: removing them makes 28 identity tests pass. This is the largest cause and the smallest change.\n2. **pandoc (17).** Replace the `shutil.which`-style presence check with a **capability probe** — execute the converter and observe the result, so an unrunnable binary is treated as absent rather than as available. Where no usable pandoc exists, the affected tests skip with a counted, reported signal. Fixing the architecture mismatch itself is out of scope here and belongs to a dependency-packaging ticket if one is warranted; this ticket makes the gate honest about it.\n3. **semgrep (8).** Redirect the settings path to a per-run scratch location. Measured: all 8 pass under a redirected `HOME`.\n4. **`ps` (1).** No remedy exists; enumeration is denied outright. Loud, counted skip.\n5. **Close gate (1).** Out of scope — `9753-647b-2f85-40ef`.\n\n### Constraints on the remedy\n\n- **Capability probes only, never environment-name heuristics.** Detection attempts the operation and observes the outcome. Username, hostname, and CI environment variables are not acceptable discriminators. This is also what keeps the remedy portable: a probe behaves correctly under any CI provider and under none, with no provider-specific trigger.\n- **No silent skips.** Every skipped test contributes to a visible count reported at session end. A test that vanishes without a counted signal is a failed outcome, because that invisibility is the exact failure mode this ticket exists to prevent.\n- **No CI coverage may be lost.** Probes succeed in CI, so every currently-running test continues to run there. The skip paths are unreachable in an environment that has the capability.\n- New fixtures or helpers may trip the **mechanism-delta ratchet** (`autouse_fixture`, `test_helper`). Where one is genuinely needed, justify it at the definition site with a `# mechanism-ok:` marker naming this ticket. Do not hand-edit the baseline.\n\n## Testing strategy\n\nTDD throughout: each cause gets a failing test written **first** that asserts the intended behaviour, then the fix.\n\n- Probe correctness is tested directly by simulating an unavailable capability and asserting the probe reports absence — including the pandoc case of a present-but-unexecutable binary, which is the case `shutil.which` gets wrong today.\n- The skip **accounting** is itself tested: a test asserts that a skipped capability produces a counted, visible signal, so the no-silent-skips property is enforced by the suite rather than by reviewer vigilance.\n- Regression evidence is the census itself: `make test` under the sandbox drops from 51 failures to the 4 owned by `0eba`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Running `make test` under the agent sandbox reports 0 failures attributable to the git-identity cause, down from 20. Verified by running `make test` under the sandbox and counting failures in that population.\n- [ ] Running `make test` under the agent sandbox reports 0 failures attributable to the semgrep cause, down from 8. Verified the same way.\n- [ ] Running `make test` under the agent sandbox reports 0 failures attributable to the pandoc cause, down from 17. Verified the same way.\n- [ ] Running `make test` under the agent sandbox reports 0 failures attributable to the `ps` cause, down from 1. Verified the same way.\n- [ ] A test asserts the pandoc probe reports \"unavailable\" when given a binary that exists on `PATH` but exits with `Errno 86`. Verified by that test passing on a host where a working pandoc is installed.\n- [ ] A test asserts each capability probe reports \"available\" on a host that has the capability, so a probe that always returns \"unavailable\" fails the suite. Verified by that test failing when the probe is stubbed to a constant.\n- [ ] Every skipped test increments a counter surfaced in the pytest run summary. Verified by a test that skips a capability and asserts the summary line reports a non-zero count.\n- [ ] No hostname, username, or CI environment variable appears in the remedy. Verified by `grep` over the diff for `CI`, `GITHUB_`, `hostname`, and `getuser`.\n- [ ] `make test` in CI executes the same test count as it does on `origin/main` today. Verified by comparing the collected count in the CI log before and after.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` reports 0 new unadmitted mechanisms. Verified by running that command.\n- [ ] After this ticket and `0eba` both land, `make verify` exits 0 under the agent sandbox. Verified by running `make verify` under the sandbox.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nThe 4 SemLock failures (`0eba-cfd8-34b0-4cb7`). The close-gate defect (`9753-647b-2f85-40ef`). Repackaging the `pypandoc` dependency for arm64 — this ticket makes the gate report the situation honestly; correcting the dependency is separable work."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789691178287995093,"uuid":"ed5193be-9ddc-428b-bd34-fb1f3f11d1d8"}